What does $300 a month become in ten years?

In an S&P 500 index fund, based on the last twenty years of returns, the typical answer is about **$66,900**. You'd have put in $36,000. The other $30,891 is money your money made. I built a tool that tells you in seconds.

## Why I built it

I read Morgan Housel's *The Psychology of Money*, and one idea stuck with me: saving steadily, and leaving room for error, matters more than picking the perfect investment. Compounding is easy to agree with and hard to *feel*.

When I looked for a tool that would let me feel it, I kept hitting the same walls. Budgeting apps wanted my bank login before showing me anything. Calculators gave one number with no story. Spreadsheets worked, but nobody wants to build one on a Sunday.

So I wrote myself a brief: a calm, friendly planner anyone can open on their phone. It should answer real questions in a few clicks, use real market history, and never ask who you are.

That became **WealthWeGo**. It's free, and it's live at [wealthwego.siiulan.dev](https://wealthwego.siiulan.dev).

